# Line endings for the deploy/infrastructure files.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (found 2026-07-31, during the deploy security hardening)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# `scripts/deploy.sh` was committed with CRLF line endings. Nobody noticed on
# Windows, where Git Bash tolerates the stray carriage returns. On the VPS —
# Linux, `core.autocrlf` unset — it is fatal, and quietly so:
#
#   scripts/deploy.sh: line 27: $'\r': command not found
#   scripts/deploy.sh: line 28: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# That second line is the dangerous one. `set -euo pipefail` FAILS, so the whole
# script then runs with errexit and pipefail OFF — every guard in it becomes
# advisory. And `scripts/deploy-remote.sh` `git reset --hard`s the checkout on
# every deploy, so a CRLF commit reaches production the moment it is pushed.
#
# The fix in the file is only half of it: a Windows editor that rewrites the
# file (or a tool defaulting to CRLF) puts it straight back, and the diff looks
# empty in most review tools. So the rule is declared here, where git enforces
# it at commit time.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROW. A repo-wide `* text=auto` would renormalise ~76 files
# across apps/ and packages/ in one commit — a real change, hidden inside an
# infrastructure fix, touching code other people are working on. Widen this only
# as a separate, intentional commit.

*.sh            text eol=lf
scripts/**      text=auto eol=lf
/Caddyfile      text eol=lf
/docker-compose.yml       text eol=lf
/docker-compose.prod.yml  text eol=lf
deploy/**       text eol=lf

# Binary-ish things under scripts/ that must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
